  "tour": {
    "title": "From Death to Resurrection: What the New Testament Promises for Your Body",
    "intro": "This tour follows the New Testament’s sequence rather than collapsing everything into “going to heaven”: Jesus’ bodily resurrection, the future resurrection of his people, the hope of being with Christ after death, and the final renewal of creation. The teachers sometimes acknowledge where Scripture gives only limited detail, so you can hear both the confidence and the restraint in the claims.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "recordId": "ask-nt-wright:podcast:0b0b5970-4d5f-11ee-955e-119eeadcb150",
        "startSec": 100,
        "endSec": 280,
        "why": "Begin here for the central distinction your question is asking about. N. T. Wright says resurrection means “actual bodies” being alive after being dead, not “the soul escaping the body,” and describes it as “life after life after death”—a sequence that separates the immediate aftermath of death from the final resurrection.",
        "episodeTitle": "#186 Has the resurrection been debunked? (Classic)",
        "sourceId": "ask-nt-wright",
        "source": "Ask NT Wright Anything",
        "audioUrl": "https://traffic.megaphone.fm/NSR9195388223.mp3?updated=1749653807",
        "durationSec": 2045,
        "adInsertionDrift": true
      },
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:8380ffa3-com-b72975ac-389f-3ffa-84fb-478fb64f2873",
        "startSec": 1360,
        "endSec": 1540,
        "why": "This clip grounds the claim in Paul’s argument in 1 Corinthians 15. The commentator connects your future to Jesus’ resurrection body: “Our resurrection body will be like Jesus’ resurrection body,” then explains why transformation is necessary—the perishable body must become fit for an imperishable life, and “the dead will be raised imperishable.”",
        "episodeTitle": "1 Corinthians 15:29-58",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/d8ngn3/1_corinthians_1529-589yhwk.mp3",
        "durationSec": 2010,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:7dbd0c70-com-ad14c96d-7e81-3bdd-a910-a23bb2f5b5f9",
        "startSec": 960,
        "endSec": 1140,
        "why": "Now hear the important qualification about what happens if you die before Christ returns. The teacher says the ultimate hope is “not to be released from the body, but to put on a new eternal supernatural body,” and then turns to Paul’s assurance that, in the meantime, death means being “at home with the Lord,” not already possessing the final resurrection body.",
        "episodeTitle": "2 Corinthians 5:1-10",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/jckmzx/2_corinthians_51-10816em.mp3",
        "durationSec": 1584,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:61fea544-com-fbfc585e-18f3-3e09-8729-7190089a2dbf",
        "startSec": 820,
        "endSec": 1000,
        "why": "This gives the public, future event that completes the sequence. Reading 1 Thessalonians 4, the commentator pictures the Lord descending with “a shout,” the archangel’s voice, and God’s trumpet; crucially, “the dead in Christ will rise first,” followed by the living. It keeps the focus on resurrection rather than an escape from embodiment.",
        "episodeTitle": "1 Thessalonians 4:13-18",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/8pb6cz/1_thess_413-189tacb.mp3",
        "durationSec": 1603,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:heaven-earth-part-3",
        "startSec": 1980,
        "endSec": 2300,
        "why": "End with the New Testament’s larger destination and with appropriate humility about the intermediate state. The speakers say that after death believers are “with Jesus,” but “it’s not the end of the story”: heaven is not the permanent destination in their reading, because the Savior comes from there “to transform everything” in new creation. They also admit that the New Testament offers only a few brief glimpses of what lies between death and resurrection.",
        "episodeTitle": "Jesus, The Ultimate Heaven & Earth Meeting Place",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee/episodes/9327a394-42a0-4eaf-a203-09e63e82aacd/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=695767b0-cd40-4e6c-ac8c-ac6bc0df77ee&awEpisodeId=9327a394-42a0-4eaf-a203-09e63e82aacd",
        "durationSec": 2514,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      }
    ],
    "closing": "Taken together, these clips present a clear shape: death is not the resurrection, and resurrection is not merely going to heaven. The New Testament’s central hope is that the God who raised Jesus will raise and transform his people bodily, while those who die beforehand are held in the presence of Christ as they await that final renewal. Where the teachers go beyond those contours, notice their qualifications—and let the bodily, world-renewing hope remain the center.",
    "totalSeconds": 1040
  },
